---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const repo = 'https://github.com/kmelve/petty-evals';
function gh(path: string) {
  return repo + '/blob/main/' + path;
}
---
<Base title="How I did this — Petty Evals" description="The methodology behind Petty Evals — a real eval pipeline for stupid questions. Wilson CIs, mode-aware pundits, reclassify-in-place. The honest version.">
  <div class="mb-12">
    <span class="sticker sticker--blue">methodology</span>
    <h1 class="display text-5xl md:text-7xl mt-3 mb-4">
      How I did this<br>
      <span style="color: var(--color-coral);">(the actual nerd shit)</span>
    </h1>
    <p class="text-lg max-w-2xl" style="color: var(--color-muted);">
      Real eval, stupid questions. This page is also a guide — if you've ever wanted to
      run your own eval against multiple models, every choice I made is here, linked to
      the actual code on GitHub. Steal liberally.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2 class="display text-3xl">The whole pipeline in one paragraph</h2>
    <p>
      For each <strong>fight</strong>, I send each of <strong>4 models</strong> a coding
      prompt <strong>60 times</strong> (3 prompt variants × 2 temperatures × 10 runs).
      A <strong>regex classifier</strong> reads each response and assigns it a label
      ("tabs", "spaces", "unknown", etc.). The model's <strong>vote</strong> is its
      modal classification across its 60 runs. The fight's <strong>verdict</strong> is
      the majority across models. Then I send each model the final tally and ask it
      to write one judgy sentence about the result. That's the pundit panel.
      One fight is 240 generations and costs about $3.
    </p>
    <p>
      Code: <a href={gh('scripts/run-evals.ts')}><code>scripts/run-evals.ts</code></a> is the entry point.
      <a href={gh('src/lib/eval/runner.ts')}><code>src/lib/eval/runner.ts</code></a> orchestrates calls.
      <a href={gh('src/lib/eval/')}><code>src/lib/eval/</code></a> has everything else.
    </p>

    <h2 class="display text-3xl">Step 1: revealed preference, not stated preference</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Don't ask the model what it prefers.</strong> "Do you prefer tabs or spaces?"
      gets you whatever it thinks you want to hear. Models are catastrophically sycophantic
      about style questions and their stated answer will not match what they actually write.
    </p>
    <p>
      Instead, ask the model to <em>do a task</em> that incidentally requires the choice.
      "Write a Python function that reverses a linked list" reveals indentation preference.
      "Write a function that compares two values and returns true if equal" reveals <code>===</code>
      vs <code>==</code>. The model picks naturally because it's focused on the task.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Vary the domain.</strong> Each fight has 3 prompt variants in different problem
      areas (data structures / strings / HTTP) so the verdict isn't a fluke of one task.
      The prompts are on each fight page — click the "exact prompts" disclosure.
    </p>
    <p>
      Prompt files live in <a href={gh('src/content/fights/')}><code>src/content/fights/</code></a>.
      Each is a TypeScript module with the question, the 3 variants, and the classifier reference.
    </p>

    <h2 class="display text-3xl">Step 2: the matrix (N=10, k=3, T=2)</h2>
    <p>
      One sample per model per fight is anecdote. The matrix turns it into data.
    </p>
    <p>
      For each fight, each model gets every prompt variant 20 times: 10 runs at temperature 0
      (mostly deterministic — same prompt, mostly same output) and 10 runs at temperature 0.7
      (default sampling — varied output). That's 60 generations per model per fight, and
      4 models means 240. Run for 24 fights (V1 lineup): 5,760 generations, ~$76, ~4 hours
      wall-clock on 4 parallel workers.
    </p>
    <p>
      Why 10 runs per cell? Because Wilson 95% CIs on n=10 with a unanimous result give a
      lower bound of ~70% — enough to meaningfully separate "this model picks X" from "this
      model sometimes picks X". I'd love n=30 per cell, but the cost goes from $76 to ~$230
      and we're testing tabs vs spaces, not COVID vaccines. Use the budget where it matters.
    </p>
    <p>
      The matrix builder is in <a href={gh('src/lib/eval/runner.ts')}><code>runner.ts</code></a>'s <code>buildPlan</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="display text-3xl">Step 3: classifiers (the load-bearing piece)</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Each fight has its own regex classifier.</strong> Not a generic "label the output" —
      a specific function that knows what to look for. <code>tabs-vs-spaces-python</code> finds
      the first indented line and checks if it starts with a tab. <code>triple-equals-js</code>
      strips comments and template literals, then counts <code>===</code> vs <code>==</code> tokens.
      <code>react-styling</code> looks for Tailwind class patterns, <code>styled.</code>, or
      <code>import './foo.css'</code>. Twenty-four classifiers, each a few dozen lines.
    </p>
    <p>
      All of them live in
      <a href={gh('src/lib/eval/classifiers/regex.ts')}><code>src/lib/eval/classifiers/regex.ts</code></a>
      — go read it, you can audit every label decision in about 15 minutes.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Two helpers fight constant battles:</strong>
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>extractCode</code> vs <code>extractAllCode</code>: most models put their code in
        triple-backtick fences, but some questions ("show me how you'd use it") provoke a SECOND
        fence with example code. If your classifier only reads the first fence, you miss signal in
        the second. I learned this mid-batch on <code>triple-equals-js</code> — GPT models put the
        impl in fence 1 (no equality operators) and the call site in fence 2 (where they wrote
        <code>n === 0</code>). The classifier said "unknown" 40 times until I patched it to
        <code>extractAllCode</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>stripJsNoise</code> / <code>stripPyNoise</code> / <code>stripPhpNoise</code>:
        strip strings and comments before counting tokens. Otherwise the <code>===</code> in
        <code>// returns true if x === y</code> gets counted as code.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>When in doubt, abstain.</strong> Classifiers return <code>'unknown'</code> rather
      than guess. False positives are way worse than missing votes — they corrupt the data without
      anyone noticing. The pundit panel can still rip on "the model couldn't decide" but not on
      "the model used three colons in a row".
    </p>

    <h2 class="display text-3xl">Step 4: stats that aren't security theater</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Wilson 95% CIs.</strong> Not normal-approximation CIs — Wilson handles small N and
      proportions near 0 or 1 without breaking. Implementation:
      <a href={gh('src/lib/eval/stats.ts')}><code>stats.ts</code></a>. 11 unit tests covering edge cases.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Shannon entropy as Disagreement Index.</strong> Normalized 0–1 across the cross-model
      vote distribution. 0 means all four models voted the same; 1 means maximum spread. The
      homepage sorts by this descending — the fight at the top is the bloodiest. I wanted a single
      number to make "this fight was actually contested" sortable.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Cohen's kappa is implemented but not (yet) computed.</strong> Honest answer: it's
      in <a href={gh('src/lib/eval/stats.ts')}><code>stats.ts</code></a>, ready to compare two
      classifier outputs (or one classifier vs a hand-labeled gold set), but I haven't hand-labeled
      a calibration set for the V1 fights. The <code>classifierKappa</code> field in every result
      JSON is <code>null</code>. That's a real gap and I'm not going to pretend otherwise. Next pass.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Verdicts are reported honestly.</strong> 2-2 ties are 2-2 ties — no tiebreaker, no
      "but if we round". Unanimous fights are unanimous. The two TS-syntax fights right now are
      legitimate 2-2 ties (interface vs type, React styling) — those are the most interesting
      results on the site.
    </p>

    <h2 class="display text-3xl">Step 5: the pundit panel</h2>
    <p>
      After all votes are in, I send each model a follow-up call. The prompt has 4 modes,
      determined by the verdict shape:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Unanimous</strong> — model is asked to be smug about being right and mock anyone
        in the wider world who picks the losing position. ("If you're still using <code>var</code>
        in 2026, I have questions about your life choices.")
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Tied</strong> — model is asked to defend its camp and dunk on the dissenting peer
        BY NAME. ("GPT-5.4 mini reaching for <code>type</code> for object shapes like it's never
        heard of declaration merging.")
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Majority with one dissenter</strong> — majority models get smug + name the dissenter;
        the dissenter gets to defend its position without apologizing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Unclear</strong> — model snarks about the messiness.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Each model gets a per-model prompt: <em>"You are Claude. You voted INTERFACE. Here's how
      your peers voted: ..."</em> Temperature is 0.8 for personality. I deliberately give the
      model a representative code sample of its own output so it remembers what it wrote.
    </p>
    <p>
      The prompts are in
      <a href={gh('src/lib/eval/pundit.ts')}><code>pundit.ts</code></a> — they're full of
      tonal examples ("a senior dev who has run out of patience", "a partisan in an internet
      flame war") because LLMs need calibration examples to actually be funny instead of
      analyst-speak.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>I never edit the quotes.</strong> Truncation at sentence boundary only. What's
      on the site is what the model said. Sometimes a quote will say something I disagree with;
      it stays.
    </p>

    <h2 class="display text-3xl">Step 6: the pattern I'd steal for my own eval</h2>
    <p>
      The thing that made this project actually shippable, not just a science fair project:
      <strong>persist raw outputs before aggregation, and write a reclassify-in-place script.</strong>
    </p>
    <p>
      Every generation gets saved to a <code>.tmp/</code> directory <em>before</em> the classifier
      runs. The classifier outputs feed into the verdict, but the raw model output is on disk.
      When I found a classifier bug mid-batch (the multi-fence issue above), I patched the
      classifier and ran
      <a href={gh('scripts/reclassify.ts')}><code>scripts/reclassify.ts</code></a>
      against the saved outputs — no API re-spend. Same trick for the
      <a href={gh('scripts/rerun-pundits.ts')}><code>rerun-pundits.ts</code></a>
      script when I rewrote the pundit prompts: load each result JSON, regenerate just the pundit
      step, patch the file. The full eval was $76. Re-rolling all the pundits cost $2.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you build an eval, do this from day one. You will find classifier bugs. You will want
      to change downstream prompts. You will be grateful you didn't have to re-pay for the
      expensive generation step.
    </p>

    <h2 class="display text-3xl">Caveats (the things I want to be honest about)</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>GPT-5.4 and GPT-5.4 mini are reasoning models.</strong> Temperature is silently
      ignored — the model picks its own sampling internally. Both temperatures (0.0 and 0.7) get
      requested for bookkeeping consistency, but in practice the GPT-5 family runs at whatever
      temperature the reasoning controller decides. I report the requested temperature; you can
      decide whether to care.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>This is four models, not all models.</strong> Adding Grok, DeepSeek, Qwen, and Opus
      is the V1.1 pass (already partially wired in
      <a href={gh('src/lib/eval/providers/index.ts')}><code>providers/index.ts</code></a> behind
      <code>enabled: false</code>).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Cohen's kappa against a hand-labeled gold set is not (yet) computed.</strong>
      See the stats section above. This is a real methodology gap.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>This is what AI models prefer, not what "developers" prefer.</strong> The training
      data includes a lot of human code, but this isn't a poll of humans. It's a poll of AIs
      about which humans they imitated. The findings can tell you about model family bias and
      training data shape; they cannot tell you what the "correct" answer to a style question is.
    </p>

    <h2 class="display text-3xl">All the code, audited by you</h2>
    <p>
      Everything is on <a href={repo}>GitHub</a>. Bring your own API keys
      (<code>ANTHROPIC_API_KEY</code>, <code>OPENAI_API_KEY</code>,
      <code>GEMINI_API_KEY</code> — see
      <a href={gh('CONTRIBUTING.md')}><code>CONTRIBUTING.md</code></a>),
      then:
    </p>
    <pre class="code-block"><code>git clone https://github.com/kmelve/petty-evals
cd petty-evals
pnpm install
pnpm evals --fight=interface-vs-type-ts --dry-run  # see the plan
pnpm evals --fight=interface-vs-type-ts            # run it for real (~$3)</code></pre>
    <p>
      Every number on this site is reproducible from the code in the repo. That's the whole point
      of an eval: if you can't run it yourself, it's not data, it's vibes.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
